This week I had the opportunity to attend ICC 2025 / ISCAS in New York as a recent MD graduate from Uzbekistan with a growing focus on coronary and cardiothoracic surgery.

For the first time, Uzbekistan was represented in the scientific program with an award-winning ePoster, which was an important moment for our local community. It showed that data and experience from a resource-limited setting can contribute to the global discussion on coronary surgery and outcomes.

Over the course of the meeting I was able to update my understanding of:
 • contemporary multi-arterial and off-pump CABG strategies;
 • minimally invasive and robotic coronary approaches;
 • the role of intraoperative and imaging-based graft assessment;
 • perioperative pathways aimed at safer and faster recovery.

I am especially grateful to the ICC faculty who made the content so educational and approachable for early-career doctors:
 • Prof. John Puskas – for emphasizing structured intraoperative quality control after CABG, including the systematic use of TTFM to assess grafts in real time;
 • Prof. David Taggart – for his consistent emphasis on evidence-based decision-making between CABG and PCI;
 • Dr. Gianluca Torregrossa – for demonstrating what is technically and practically possible with minimally invasive and robotic coronary surgery;

My goal now is to translate what I learned into everyday practice in Uzbekistan – in the operating room, ICU and journal clubs – and to encourage more students and young doctors from my country to engage with international cardiac surgery meetings.

Many thanks to the ICC 2025 / ISCAS organising committee and the entire faculty for an excellent, truly international program.

